@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Wrench private transport prototype
+
+This tree is a feasibility prototype. It is not qualified for production sending.
+
+## Provenance
+
+- Wacli baseline: `openclaw/wacli` v0.13.0 commit `1e15f646d23598ef5db2bdb4659ac39cc5188ad2`.
+- Whatsmeow baseline: commit `85d99080dee8ebd10ca7021751fe432d880d3b76`, resolved by Wacli as `v0.0.0-20260716095330-85d99080dee8`.
+- Checked Whatsmeow patch: `patches/whatsmeow-85d99080dee8-wrench-private.patch`.
+- Patch SHA-256: `cbf6f0b72963b365ca4c81f86993cf8a77a7eec6ffcd973d13aa68fe7331fcdc`.
+- Validation toolchain: Go 1.25.12 darwin/arm64 archive with SHA-256 `fa2c88bbcf64bd3b2aef355f026cfec6d3a4a01c132f999c8f8c964eb767164f`.
+
+The standard build does not expose a working private mutation backend. A usable prototype build requires both the checked dependency patch and the `wrench_private_transport` build tag. `scripts/test-wrench-private-transport.sh` creates a disposable patched dependency copy, uses a temporary Go module replacement, tests both layers, and builds the tagged binary.
+
+## Private request boundary
+
+`wacli wrench-private send` accepts one strict JSON value through stdin or `--input` pointing to a regular, non-symlink file with mode exactly `0600`. The command has no recipient or message-body flags and accepts no positional arguments. The body is never placed in argv or an environment variable. The response contains hashes and proof state, not the recipient, message ID, or body.
+
+The follow-mode daemon creates an owner-only Unix socket, a 32-byte owner-only HMAC key, and an atomically written owner-only status file in the selected Wacli store. Every request and response is HMAC-SHA256 authenticated. A request binds all of these values:
+
+- protocol descriptor hash
+- executable hash
+- store subject hash
+- authenticated WhatsApp subject hash
+- daemon PID and start time
+- post-handshake connection epoch
+- request nonce and issuance time
+
+The daemon rejects stale timestamps, replayed nonces, a full replay cache, changed bindings, and connection-epoch drift. `wrench-private status` uses the same authenticated channel and performs a provider barrier before reporting idle or reconciling an uncertain send.
+
+The accepted send contract is deliberately narrow: one canonical non-device DM, LID, or group JID; one 1 to 16 KiB text body without leading or trailing whitespace; `no_preview: true`; `no_retry: true`; and a timeout from 100 ms through 45 seconds. Replies, mentions, media, link previews, disappearing-message metadata, and status/channel sends are rejected.
+
+## Mutation ownership
+
+Only one mutation worker may exist for the daemon connection epoch. A client timeout returns `still_in_flight`; it does not cancel ownership or permit another send. The worker retains the route lane until it quiesces. Any possibly submitted result that lacks the complete proof poisons the lane. There is no automatic resend.
+
+An exact recovery requires all of the following on the original store and connection epoch:
+
+1. The worker has quiesced.
+2. A bounded Whatsmeow keepalive IQ with namespace `w:p`, type `get`, destination `types.ServerJID`, and retry disabled receives a valid response on that connection.
+3. Every registered non-response handler whose receive sequence is at or before that response has returned.
+4. Synchronous Wacli event handling has reported no observed store or persistence failure.
+5. The exact generated outbound message ID is present in the same Wacli store and its body hash matches.
+
+The final proof returns the keepalive response sequence and a deterministic hash of the committed row fields. A reconnect changes the epoch and fails closed. Queue overflow, escaped or late handlers, event-handler failure, store transaction failure, a blackholed or half-open connection, timeout, and epoch drift also fail closed.
+
+## Deterministic evidence
+
+Run the complete prototype check without connecting an account:
+
+```bash
+GO_BIN=/path/to/go1.25.12/bin/go \
+  bash scripts/test-wrench-private-transport.sh
+```
+
+The test suite uses fake providers and no live WhatsApp session. It covers successful proof, exact keepalive construction, handler drain ordering, queue overflow, handler escape, handler or transaction failure, reconnect, late handler completion, caller timeout with a still-running provider worker, permanent no-retry behavior, lane poisoning, exact committed-read recovery, a blackholed barrier, and epoch drift. No live message was sent while building or validating this prototype.
+
+## Production qualification gaps
+
+- The patch reaches into Whatsmeow connection and handler internals. It needs upstream review or a maintained fork, compatibility checks for every dependency update, and integration tests against a controlled WhatsApp test account before production use.
+- The deterministic tests do not exercise a real Noise socket, a real provider acknowledgement, server-side duplicate behavior, or the full reconnect lifecycle. They prove the local state machine, not delivery semantics offered by WhatsApp.
+- Wacli store failures are surfaced through synchronous event-handler status and known `store_failed` or `persist_failed` warning codes. Existing best-effort writes that discard errors are not fully instrumented. Production qualification requires transaction-level success reporting for every database mutation reachable from a sequenced handler.
+- The returned committed revision is a deterministic hash of the row read after synchronous store calls return. Wacli does not currently expose a database commit sequence or snapshot revision. A production proof should use a store-owned monotonic commit revision in the same read transaction.
+- Whatsmeow's existing long-handler escape threshold is five minutes. The Wrench barrier has its own shorter deadline and fails closed first, but production should use an explicit bounded handler contract instead of inheriting that threshold.
+- Work started by an event handler after the synchronous handler returns is outside the receive barrier. Production qualification must either prohibit such persistence or register and drain it explicitly.
+- Socket permissions and HMAC authentication were tested locally. Peer-credential checks, key rotation, crash recovery, filesystem ownership changes, multiple local users, suspend/resume, and daemon replacement still need adversarial tests.
+- An uncertain send with no matching committed row remains poisoned for the life of the connection epoch. The prototype does not query a provider-side idempotency ledger because WhatsApp exposes no qualified one here.
+- The payload contract omits common message forms. Each additional form needs an exact canonicalization and committed-read proof before it can be enabled.
